# /spec — Write a Specification

Write a detailed specification for the described feature and save it to
`specs/<feature>/spec.md`.

## Instructions

1. Ask the user for the feature name if it is not already provided.
2. Create the directory `specs/<feature>/` if it does not exist.
3. Write `specs/<feature>/spec.md` using the template below.
4. Every requirement **must** have a unique ID in the form `REQ-NNN`
   (three zero-padded digits, e.g. `REQ-001`).
5. Do **not** begin implementation. The spec stage is for requirements only.
6. After writing, remind the user to run:
   ```
   sddkit check <feature>
   sddkit approve <feature> spec
   ```

## spec.md Template

```markdown
# Spec: <Feature Name>

## Overview
<!-- One paragraph describing what this feature does and why. -->

## Requirements

| ID | Requirement | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| REQ-001 | ... | High |
| REQ-002 | ... | Medium |
| REQ-003 | ... | Low |

## Out of Scope
<!-- Explicitly list what this feature does NOT do. -->

## Open Questions
<!-- List any unresolved questions that must be answered before implementation. -->

## Acceptance Criteria
<!-- High-level conditions under which the feature is "done". -->
```

## Constraints
- IDs must be unique within the spec. The checker (`sddkit check`) will flag duplicates.
- Do not start plan, tasks, or implementation until this spec is approved.
